<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Culloden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1746">1746</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Culloden · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Culloden was fought on 16 April 1746 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a>, where the government army under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover">Duke of Cumberland</a> destroyed the Jacobite army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a> in less than an hour. It was the last pitched battle fought on British soil. Harsh repression followed, and laws against Highland dress, arms and hereditary jurisdictions broke the power of the clans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-culloden.jpg" alt="Battle of Culloden" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Morier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Culloden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a> landed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Western Isles</a> on 23 July 1745 to reclaim the British throne for the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">House of Stuart</a>. After a victory at Prestonpans in September the Jacobites controlled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and their invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> before retreating. Recruitment brought their strength to more than 8,000, and on 17 January 1746 they repulsed a government force under Henry Hawley at the Battle of Falkirk Muir. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover">Duke of Cumberland</a> took over command, the Jacobites abandoned the siege of Stirling Castle and withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a>.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> blockade left them short of money and food. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> resumed the campaign on 8 April, Charles and his advisers agreed their best option was a set-piece battle. On the night of 15 April Lord George Murray led a march to attack the government camp at Nairn, but it was slow and confused, and he turned back before reaching it, leaving the army tired and hungry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 16 April the Jacobites, about 5,400 strong, formed on Drummossie Moor with the Highland regiments in the first line and the River Nairn about a kilometre to their right. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a>'s army marched across country and formed line within about 500 metres. The artillery of both sides opened fire at about 1:30 pm, though accounts differ on how long the Jacobites waited under bombardment.</p><p>When Charles ordered the advance, government gunners switched to canister shot, and the Highland regiments came on into heavy fire. In the centre and on the right the regiments swerved and became entangled. Many reached the government lines, where the brunt fell on Barrell's 4th Foot and Dejean's 37th Foot, but a second line counterattacked and surrounded them on three sides. The Jacobite first line collapsed, French regulars covered the retreat, and the fleeing Highlanders were pursued by dragoons. Jacobite losses were between 1,500 and 2,000 killed or wounded, many in the pursuit. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a> reported 50 dead and 259 wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The army was ordered to disperse until Charles returned with French support, and by 18 April most of it had disbanded, though at least a third had missed the battle. Of 3,471 prisoners, only one in twenty was tried; about 1,200 were transported, 905 were released under the Indemnity Act 1747, 362 were exchanged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and 120 were executed. The defeat effectively ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobitism">Jacobitism</a> as a significant element in British politics.</p><p>Commemoration was slight before the 1840s. A cairn was raised on the battlefield in 1881, and the Gaelic Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a> began an annual ceremony in 1925. The site is now protected, with a visitor centre opened in December 2007 and the farmhouse of Leanach standing where a cottage probably served as a government field hospital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Culloden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jacobite risings in support of the exiled Stuarts were defeated in 1715 and in 1745–1746, when Charles Edward Stuart reached Derby before his army was destroyed at the <b>Battle of Culloden</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…flight of Prince Charles Edward Stuart, &quot;Bonnie Prince Charlie&quot;, from Benbecula to the Isle of Skye as he evaded government soldiers after his defeat at the <b>Battle of Culloden</b> in 1746: disguised as a serving maid, he escaped in a small boat with the help of Flora MacDonald.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-skye-boat-song">The Skye Boat Song</a></li><li><blockquote>The rising ended at <b>Culloden</b> in April 1746.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-6">6 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jacobite rising he launched ended in defeat at <b>Culloden</b> in April 1746.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-23">23 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
